#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060922 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a} & ={a}_{1}\\mathbf{i}+{a}_{2}\\mathbf{j}+{a}_{3}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\mathbf{b} & ={b}_{1}\\mathbf{i}+{b}_{2}\\mathbf{j}+{b}_{3}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe cross product $\\mathbf{a}\\times\\mathbf b$ can be calculated using the following determinant:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \n\t\\left|\n\t\t{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3}\\end{array}}\n\t\\right|\n$$\n\nFor instance, suppose that we have the two vectors $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle 2,-1,5 \\rangle$, $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle -1,4,2 \\rangle$. We can find the cross product of $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 2-5\\cdot 4)\\mathbf{i}-(2\\cdot 2-5\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}+(2\\cdot 4-(-1)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-22\\mathbf{i}-9\\mathbf{j}+7\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-22,-9,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060923 :tutorial/content "We have been using the following formula to compute the cross product of two vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{2} & {b}_{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}{a}_{1} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =({a}_{2}{b}_{3}-{a}_{3}{b}_{2})\\mathbf{i}-({a}_{1}{b}_{3}-{a}_{3}{b}_{1})\\mathbf{j}+({a}_{1}{b}_{2}-{a}_{2}{b}_{1})\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from? Let's find out.\n\nSuppose we want to find the cross product $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}$ for $\\mathbf{a}=a_1\\mathbf{i}+a_2\\mathbf{j}+a_3\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=b_1\\mathbf{i}+b_2\\mathbf{j}+b_3\\mathbf{k}.$ Using properties of the cross product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =({a}_{1}\\mathbf{i}+{a}_{2}\\mathbf{j}+{a}_{3}\\mathbf{k})\\times ({b}_{1}\\mathbf{i}+{b}_{2}\\mathbf{j}+{b}_{3}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1})\\mathbf{i}\\times \\mathbf{i}+({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2})\\mathbf{i}\\times \\mathbf{j}+({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{3})\\mathbf{i}\\times \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & +({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{1})\\mathbf{j}\\times \\mathbf{i}+({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{2})\\mathbf{j}\\times \\mathbf{j}+({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{3})\\mathbf{j}\\times \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & +({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{1})\\mathbf{k}\\times \\mathbf{i}+({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{2})\\mathbf{k}\\times \\mathbf{j}+({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{3})\\mathbf{k}\\times \\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the cross product of two parallel (collinear) vectors is equal to $\\mathbf 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{i}\\times\\mathbf{i} = \\mathbf{j}\\times\\mathbf{j} = \\mathbf{k}\\times\\mathbf{k} = \\mathbf 0.\n$$\n\nAlso, using the definition of the cross product we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{i}\\times \\mathbf{j}=\\mathbf{k}, & \\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{j}\\times \\mathbf{i}=-\\mathbf{k}, \\\\ \\mathbf{j}\\times \\mathbf{k}=\\mathbf{i}, & \\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{k}\\times \\mathbf{j}=-\\mathbf{i}, \\\\ \\mathbf{k}\\times \\mathbf{i}=\\mathbf{j}, & \\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{i}\\times \\mathbf{k}=-\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1})\\underbrace{\\mathbf{i}\\times \\mathbf{i}}_{=\\mathbf{0}}+({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2})\\underbrace{\\mathbf{i}\\times \\mathbf{j}}_{=\\mathbf{k}}+({a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{3})\\underbrace{\\mathbf{i}\\times \\mathbf{k}}_{=-\\mathbf{j}} \\\\  & +({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{1})\\underbrace{\\mathbf{j}\\times \\mathbf{i}}_{=-\\mathbf{k}}+({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{2})\\underbrace{\\mathbf{j}\\times \\mathbf{j}}_{=\\mathbf{0}}+({a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{3})\\underbrace{\\mathbf{j}\\times \\mathbf{k}}_{=\\mathbf{i}} \\\\  & +({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{1})\\underbrace{\\mathbf{k}\\times \\mathbf{i}}_{=\\mathbf{j}}+({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{2})\\underbrace{\\mathbf{k}\\times \\mathbf{j}}_{=-\\mathbf{i}}+({a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{3})\\underbrace{\\mathbf{k}\\times \\mathbf{k}}_{=\\mathbf{0}} \\\\  & =({a}_{2}{b}_{3}-{a}_{3}{b}_{2})\\mathbf{i}-({a}_{1}{b}_{3}-{a}_{3}{b}_{1})\\mathbf{j}+({a}_{1}{b}_{2}-{a}_{2}{b}_{1})\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression above is equal to the formula given for the cross product of two general vectors using the determinant." :tutorial/title "Proof of the Formula for the Cross Product Using the Determinant"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060931 :tutorial/content "The **determinant** of a general $2 \\times 2$ matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{a}} & {\\color{red}{b}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{c}} & {\\color{blue}{d}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nis denoted $\\text {det} ({A})$ and computed as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text {det} ({A}) = \\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{a}} & {\\color{red}{b}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{c}} & {\\color{blue}{d}}\\end{vmatrix} =  {\\color{blue}ad} - {\\color{red}bc}.\n$$\n\nFor example, given the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\n\t\\begin{bmatrix}{\\color{blue}{2}} & {\\color{red}{1}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{5}} & {\\color{blue}{3}}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ \nthe determinant is\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{blue}{2}} \\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}} - {\\color{red}{1}} \\cdot {\\color{red}{5}} = 1.\n$$\n\nTo remember how to compute the determinant of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, you can imagine taking the product of the diagonal of the matrix, and then subtracting the product of the off-diagonal terms, as follows:\n\n![](images/25/2594f99e0f02241dc6d01f85c82923103df1d6fb4dda5ca03d438637f5178a28.png)\n\nLater, we will see that the determinant is related to the \"volume\" spanned by the row vectors or column vectors in a matrix. We will also see that the determinant can tell us about many other properties of a matrix. \n\nHowever, in this lesson, we will just focus on computing the determinant." :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060969 :tutorial/content "Consider the region below, which represents the inequality $2 < x < 5.$\n\n![](images/fe/feeae0bfa9b29e6f034a3fd33d1e9fbd369840835af2d6588892ee6edc2fa120.png)\n\nWe can represent this inequality using **interval notation** as follows:\n\n$$\n(2,5)\n$$\n\nThe parentheses $(\\phantom{0})$ tell us that the endpoints $x=2$ and $x=5$ are *not* included in this interval.\n\nWhen an interval does not include the endpoints, we say that it is **open**. \n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in (2,5).\n$$\n\nThe symbol $\\in$ means \"lies in\" or \"belongs to.\" So, the above reads, \"*$x$ belongs to the (open) interval $(2,5).$*\"" :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060970 :tutorial/content "Some intervals may include one parenthesis and one bracket. \n\nFor example, consider the following interval:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{{\\big[}}} 1,4 {\\color{red}{\\big)}}\n$$\n\nTo express this interval as an inequality, we note the following:\n\n- The left bracket $\\,\\color{blue}[\\,$ tells us that left endpoint $1$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{blue}{\\leq}}$ for this endpoint.\n\n- The right parenthesis $\\,\\color{red})\\,$ tells us that the right endpoint $4$ is *not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use ${\\color{red}{<      }}\\,$ for this endpoint.\n\nTherefore, the inequality that corresponds to the given interval is \n\n$$\n1 \\,\\,{\\color{blue}{\\leq  }}\\,\\,   x \\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\lt}}\\,\\, 4.\n$$\n\nThe interval can be graphed as follows:\n\n![](images/5a/5a67872e8193c2bcc249a9a9006acb916909099e4babdbe5cdfa6d2643c10ad2.png)\n\nIntervals with one bracket and one parenthesis are called **half-open** (or **half-closed**)." :tutorial/title "Half-Open Intervals"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060973 :tutorial/content "Now consider the region below, which represents the inequality $-3 \\leq x \\leq 1.$\n\n![](images/38/38c5bf5408bbbeb0f63e198e0564a37e8fe6a34d7f2ce340751d8000443123ed.png)\n\nWe express this inequality using interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n[-3,1]\n$$\n\nThe brackets $[\\phantom{0}]$ tell us that the endpoints $x=-3$ and $x=1$ *are* included in this interval.\n\nWhen an interval includes the endpoints, we say that it is **closed**. \n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [-3,1].\n$$\n\nThe above reads, \"*$x$ belongs to the (closed) interval $[-3,1].$*\"" :tutorial/title "Closed Intervals"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186061006 :tutorial/content "How do we find the determinant of a $3\\times3$ matrix such as\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nOne way to do this is to take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as shown below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{red}{7}} & {\\color{red}{1}} & {\\color{red}{-3}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{2}} & {\\color{blue}{4}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{5}} & {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{-1}}\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{red}{7}}\\hspace{0.167em})}_{{\\color{red}{{a}_{11}}}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{2}} & {\\color{blue}{4}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{0}} & {\\color{blue}{-1}}\\end{vmatrix}}^{{\\color{blue}{{M}_{11}}}}-\\underbrace{(\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{red}{1\\hspace{0.167em}}})}_{{\\color{red}{{a}_{12}}}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{4}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{5}} & {\\color{blue}{-1}}\\end{vmatrix}}^{{\\color{blue}{{M}_{12}}}}+\\underbrace{({\\color{red}{-3}})}_{{\\color{red}{{a}_{13}}}}\\cdot \\overbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}{\\color{blue}{3}} & {\\color{blue}{2}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{5}} & {\\color{blue}{0}}\\end{vmatrix}}^{{\\color{blue}{{M}_{13}}}} \\\\  & =7(-2-0)-1(-3-20)-3(0-10) \\\\  & =-14+23+30 \\\\  & =39\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the above, we have denoted $\\color{blue}M_{1j}$ as the minor of the entry ${\\color{red}a_{1j}}.$\n\nIn general, the determinant of a $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = {\\color{red}a_{11}}{\\color{blue}M_{11}} - {\\color{red}a_{12}}{\\color{blue}M_{12}} + {\\color{red}a_{13}}{\\color{blue}M_{13}}.\n$$\n\nThat is, if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{bmatrix},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i\\end{vmatrix} & =a\\begin{vmatrix}e & f \\\\ h & i\\end{vmatrix}-b\\begin{vmatrix}d & f \\\\ g & i\\end{vmatrix}+c\\begin{vmatrix}d & e \\\\ g & h\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =a(ei-fh)-b(di-fg)+c(dh-eg).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important:** Do not forget about the *minus sign* in front of the *second term* in the formula!" :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186061007 :tutorial/content "If the determinant of a square matrix $A$ is equal to zero, then we say that $A$ is a **singular** matrix.\n\nIf a matrix is singular, then it does not have a multiplicative inverse." :tutorial/title "Singular Matrices"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186061008 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the $3\\times 3$ matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ k & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nthat has an unknown constant $k$ as one of the entries, and we are given that $A$ is singular. How do we find all the possible values of $k?$\n\nOne way is to use the formula for the determinant of a $3 \\times 3$ matrix to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k,$ and then make an equation with the given determinant to solve for $k.$\n\nSo first, let's use the formula for the determinant on our matrix to get an expression in terms of $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ k & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}k & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}k & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1-2)-2(k-0)+3(2k-0) \\\\  & =-3-2k+6k \\\\  & =4k-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since we are told that $\\det(A) =0,$ let's equate the above expression to $0$ and solve for $k\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ k & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ 4k-3 & =0 \\\\ 4k & =3 \\\\ k & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only possible value for $k$ is $\\dfrac{3}{4}.$" :tutorial/title "Solving for a Variable"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065372 :tutorial/content "To compute the integral of any power function $x^n,$ where $n \\neq -1,$ we can use the **power rule for integration**. The power rule for integration states that\n\n$$\n\\int x^n\\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac {x^{n + 1}}{n + 1} + C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is any constant and $n\\neq -1.$ For example, in the case of $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{3+1}}{3+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the **antiderivative** of $f'(x) = x^3$ is $f(x) = \\dfrac {x^{4}}{4}+C .$ \n\nWe can check that this is the correct answer by taking the derivative of $f(x)$ and seeing that it is indeed equal to $x^3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{x}^{4})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4{x}^{4-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{3}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the constant $C$ disappeared when we differentiated because the derivative of any constant is zero. We always add $+C$ whenever we find the antiderivative of a function because we know that it will disappear when we differentiate.\n\n**Note:** The power rule for integration is just the power rule for differentiation in reverse!\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} \\left( x^{n+1} \\right) = (n+1)x^n \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad \\displaystyle \\int x^n \\, \\textrm dx = \\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1}\n$$" :tutorial/title "The Power Rule for Integration"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065405 :tutorial/content "An **antiderivative** is the *opposite* of a derivative. For example:\n\n- Using the power rule, we know that the *derivative* of $\\color{blue}x^2$ is ${\\color{red}2x}.$\n\n- Going in the *opposite* direction, we say that an **antiderivative** of $\\color{red}2x$ is ${\\color{blue}x^2}.$\n\nFor any given function, there are infinitely many antiderivatives. For example:\n\n- The derivative of $\\color{blue}x^2$ is ${\\color{red}2x},$ so an *antiderivative* of $\\color{red}2x$ is ${\\color{blue}x^2}.$\n\n- The derivative of $\\color{blue}x^2+1$ is ${\\color{red}2x},$ so another *antiderivative* of $\\color{red}2x$ is ${\\color{blue}x^2+1}.$\n\n- The derivative of $\\color{blue}x^2+2$ is ${\\color{red}2x},$ so another *antiderivative* of $\\color{red}2x$ is ${\\color{blue}x^2+2}.$\n\n- ...\n\nIn general, all the antiderivatives of $\\color{red}2x$ can be written in the form ${\\color{blue}x^2+C},$ where $C$ is a constant.\n\nTo represent the antiderivative symbolically, we use a special symbol $\\displaystyle \\int$ called the **integral** symbol. So, using **integral notation**, we can write\n\n$$\n\\int 2x \\, \\textrm d x = x^2 + C\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ represents an arbitrary constant and is called the **constant of integration**.\n\nIn words, $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 2x \\, \\textrm d x\n$$ is said as “the integral of $2x$ with respect to $x$” or “the antiderivative of $2x$.” Integrals like this are called **indefinite integrals**, and the function being **integrated** ($2x$ in this case) is called the **integrand**." :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065406 :tutorial/content "Let's recap the main points:\n\n- Integrals like $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x^3\\,\\textrm d x\n$$ are called **indefinite integrals**.\n\n- Whenever we calculate an indefinite integral, we must always remember to add an **arbitrary constant** $+C.$\n\n- To integrate power functions, we can use the **power rule for integration**, which states that\n\n$$\n\\int x^n\\,\\textrm d x = \\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} + C,\n$$\n\nprovided that $n \\neq -1.$ To emphasize, the power rule does *not* work when $n=-1.$ So we cannot use the above formula to work out $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$ Integrals like this need to be handled differently, and you'll find out how soon." :tutorial/title "Some Final Notes on Indefinite Integrals and Constants"}]}
